 "review": {"identity": "swe2-max-1", "method": "Acceptance suite run locally with `retail,gpu,audio` (CS_GAME_DIR, CS_ENGINE_IMAGE): the five synthetic tests and the two retail tests of accept_m01_convoy_rocket_ drive the production launch -> fuse -> resolver path against composed M01 — the retail half selects t_truck01, t_truck02 and t_truck03 through the production targeting session and destroys each with one routed rocket on its own targets.zrd node, and the no-selection refusal fires no rocket. The owner-command smoke `cs --cs-path $CS_GAME_DIR --mission M01 --smoke-seconds 45 --capture-dir private/evidence/BUG-M01-CONVOY-ROCKET-DAMAGE` then ran the same production path on this machine's GPU (Apple M3 Pro, Metal): its Rocket phase places the player ahead of a declared hostile ground target, selects it through the Ctrl+R edge and fires `X`; the run's own report.json records the new `weapon_rocket_damages` check passed — the proximity detonation routed 2 hit(s) to t_truck01 on actor 1:9 and destroyed it — inside 72 checks with no failures, and the framebuffer PNGs (the rocket-burst frame shows the detonation over the convoy on the bridge) are hashed here as artifacts. The run is automated, never `human_play`, and no original executable ran. This harness derives every field from the recorded log, production discovery of $CS_GAME_DIR, the smoke's report and the hashes of those artifacts. LIMITS: the selection is the mission's declared scripted-select (the original's lock gesture is unmeasured), the rocket's tuning is the designed m01-play-weapons.designed-rocket record rather than the original's, and the stack holds six units of two each so three rockets is all a launch carries. Validated with tools/validate_evidence.py --require-pass."},
 "claim": "implemented"
